 2. New effort to oust Cook 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The Supreme Court said President Trump could not fire Fed governor Cook without first giving her a chance to defend herself.

 In recent months, Trump has been putting a formal process in place to address that procedural shortcoming. The latest: A new memo shows that Trump is establishing a three-person committee to investigate the allegations against Cook and hold a hearing next month.

 Why it matters: Trump is reviving his effort to remove the central bank official — potentially setting up another legal showdown over a question the Supreme Court left unresolved: whether the allegations against her constitute sufficient grounds for dismissal.

 Zoom in: The committee will investigate the allegations against Cook and determine whether there is "cause" for her removal.

 The three-person committee will include White House economic adviser Kevin Hassett, Equal Employment Opportunity Commission chair Andrea Lucas and acting Office of Government Ethics director Keith Sonderling. What to watch: The memo says that Cook must appear at a closed-door hearing on Nov. 5 to present evidence and respond to questions.

 Cook has an opportunity to submit a final written response by Nov. 10, the memo says. Then the group will recommend to Trump whether there are grounds to fire her. The other side: Lawyers for Cook said in a statement today to Axios that "in light of the President's repeated statements that he has already decided to fire Governor Cook — regardless of the facts and evidence — we have grave doubts that this 'hearing' is a legitimate one that would conform to the law."

 "We have engaged with the White House to evaluate whether the process proposed has a possibility of being a genuine one and not simply a box checking exercise." Flashback: Trump moved to fire Cook last summer over allegations that she misrepresented information on mortgage applications, an unprecedented attempt to remove a Fed official that escalated to the Supreme Court.

 Cook denied wrongdoing and challenged her dismissal in court, arguing that the president lacked legal grounds to remove her. Since then, the administration sent her a letter formally notifying her that Trump was considering her removal, giving her 21 days to respond to the allegations.

 Cook submitted a response denying the allegations and arguing there was no legal basis to fire her. The bottom line: The outcome could have implications well beyond Cook, testing the limits of presidential power over an independent central bank.

 Trump has also renewed calls for former Fed chair Jerome Powell to resign from the Fed board, despite an independent watchdog report finding no criminal wrongdoing in the Fed's costly headquarters renovation. The Justice Department said last week it would not reopen its investigation . 
 
